\value{
\code{go_*()} functions return a graph object;
  \code{add_*()} functions return the modified figure.
}
\description{
The ohlc (short for Open-High-Low-Close) is a style of financial chart
describing open, high, low and close for a given `x` coordinate (most
likely time). The tip of the lines represent the `low` and `high` values
and the horizontal segments represent the `open` and `close` values.
Sample points where the close value is higher (lower) than the open value
are called increasing (decreasing). By default, increasing items are
drawn in green whereas decreasing are drawn in red.
}
\details{
\code{go_ohlc()} creates a validated graph object (a named list).
\code{add_ohlc()} creates one and adds it to a figure.


Nested objects are given as named lists or with their constructors, which
are linked from the arguments below. Every property is checked against
the plotly.js schema when the object is created or modified (see
\code{\link{plotly_obj-modify}}). One-sided formulas (\code{~col}) are
evaluated in the data, and then checked, when the trace is added with
\code{add_<trace>()} or \code{add_trace()}.
}
\examples{
d <- data.frame(day = 1:30, close = EuStockMarkets[1:30, "DAX"])
d$open <- c(d$close[1], head(d$close, -1))
d$high <- pmax(d$open, d$close) + 5
d$low <- pmin(d$open, d$close) - 5
d |>
  go_figure() |>
  add_ohlc(
    x = ~day,
    open = ~open,
    high = ~high,
    low = ~low,
    close = ~close
  )
}
\seealso{
\code{\link{go_figure}()}, \code{\link{add_trace}()}
}
